    role="img"
    aria-label="Plate 3 of 4: the autoresearch loop, tuned against the harness's own corpus — all shipped. A benchmark substrate runs each scenario as a per-run trace; the corpus is split into a train slice of 25 scenarios, whose traces the proposer may read, and a sealed holdout slice of 10 scenarios used for scores only. The mutation surface is a registry of six tunable component families: system prompt, tool description, compaction threshold, stuck detector, recovery injection, and hook config. The loop runs in order. One, ParentSampler draws a parent config from the archive — epsilon-greedy over a Pareto front, so rejected branches stay reachable. Two, the Proposer, a stronger model, reads failed TRAIN traces and emits one GEPA-style change with a rationale. Three, run_experiment applies that single-component mutation in an isolated git worktree and scores it — Welch's t-test on TRAIN, non-regression on the sealed HOLDOUT with Bonferroni correction. Four, interpret returns one verdict: ADOPT, HOLD, REJECT, or SKIP. Five, adopt is automatic — not an approval gate — writing a clean win as a git commit that reverts in one command; each adoption is logged to audit.jsonl as a ComponentMutated event. Six, the Mutation Archive, a sqlite store, records the id, parent, component, diff, train and holdout scores and p-value of every attempt, and feeds ParentSampler to close the loop. Seven, a daily report surfaces HELD, inconclusive wins for asynchronous review; there is no every-N-promotions human gate. Two read-only guards sit beside the loop and never halt it: BudgetController is a circuit breaker on wallclock, iterations and token window, and the Eval Sentinel watches overfit gap, near-duplicates and saturation to guard the holdout. A clean adopt becomes the committed New Baseline for the next iteration. Solid edges are loop order; dashed edges are feedback."
